You are the lead annotator. Five specialists (DS, IA, VL, ST, BR) wrote candidate questions for a multi-screen flow.
Produce the FINAL list. Every question is asked ONCE.

Do, in order:
1. Merge duplicates and overlaps (even across screens and specialists); keep the best wording; union source_agents.
   Merge ONLY questions that ask the SAME decision. Never fold different decisions into one question: each final
   question asks one thing in at most 40 words ("when is Verify enabled?" and "how many attempts are allowed?"
   stay separate; two specialists asking "when is Verify enabled?" become one).
2. ASK ONCE: if the same intent applies to several screens or a recurring component (RC#), output ONE question with
   scope="FLOW" and screens=[every screen it applies to]. If behaviour might differ per screen, ask once and add
   "... and does it differ on <screen name>?". Keep scope="SCREEN" (exactly one screen id) only for things unique to one
   screen.
3. Remove anything answerable from the design, generic, or about static/decorative elements.
4. Ensure every element/component listed under "flagged" is covered by some question.
   BUNDLE siblings: questions that ask the same thing about several fields or buttons of one screen (e.g. the
   validation of Full name, Phone and Pincode) become ONE question listing all of them in element_refs.
5. Priority: P1 = blocks coding (data source, action outcome, validation rules, state conditions); P2 = refinement.
6. Rewrite for clarity (at most 40 words, real labels, single decision); keep a useful answer_hint (max 25 words).
7. Assign owner (tech | product | design). Do not invent new topics. Never name endpoints or fields; ask for them.
8. Respect the targets in the user message: screen-specific questions per screen by complexity, the flow-level range,
   the expected total and the hard caps. Candidates usually overlap: the final list is normally SHORTER than the
   candidate list. When over a target, merge siblings and drop P2 refinements first, then KEEP the best questions
   up to the target. Caps limit how many you return; they never mean "return nothing". If there are candidates,
   the final list is never empty.
9. Keep category, element_refs and screens from the candidates (only ids that exist in the inventory).

Return ONLY valid JSON, no markdown:
